// `@FR-O-Buffer` — a local rebound from a call that builds into the caller's POOLED buffer
// keeps that buffer alive across the rebind, on the interpreter as on native.
//
// `x = a ?? mk(i)` in a loop: the call arm's lift is rebound every pass, and the hidden
// buffer it hands `mk` is minted once and re-entered through `OpClear` (one store per site
// per activation).  `mk` builds its record INTO that buffer and hands it back, so after the
// first pass the lift holds the buffer's store.  The interpreter freed the store the lift was
// displacing BEFORE the call — unconditionally — while native frees it after the call and only
// when the new value names another store.  On the second pass the pre-free released the pooled
// buffer under the callee, the callee wrote its record into a freed store, and the next store
// minted (here by `note`'s text and byte work) took the slot: the drop hook then read the
// bytes of whatever landed there — `alive2,2,alive3,8` for `alive2,2,alive3,3`, garbage under
// `LOFT_POISON=1`.  The Set now takes the guarded post-free (`OpFreeRefIfDistinct` of the old
// store against the new one), the identity test the exit free and native's rebind already use.
// The callee is spelled as a BARE TAIL on purpose: that road built into the buffer before
// `(R-Rebind)` extended it to `return`-ending bodies, so this cell fails on the build before
// the fix whatever the switch says.  The drop hook's trace is the channel, as in
// `a-join-bound-local-owns-what-it-was-handed`.
//
// @falsified-at: 185e8fd54 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0; native INERT (expected for a
//   backend-divergence guard — only one side can move)
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/a-pooled-buffer-outlives-the-reassign-of-the-local-it-fills.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0,
//   interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0; native INERT (expected for a backend-divergence guard — only one side can move)
// CHANNEL: the assertion count and the exit code on the interpreter; native holds, because its
//   rebind already frees the displaced store by identity after the call.
// ARMED: no instrument — each hook call is written to this guard's own trace file; `LOFT_POISON=1`
//   turns the stale id into garbage but is not needed for the cell to move.
// WITNESS: on the control the second pass drains `alive3,8` (a freed record read back through
//   whatever `note` minted into its slot) where the fix drains `alive3,3`.
// HOLDS: leak and panic are unmoved on both trees; native answers the same on both.

fn trace_path() -> text { return "loft_pooled_buffer_trace.tmp"; }
fn note(s: text) {
  f = file(trace_path());
  cur = f.content() ?? "";
  if cur != "" { cur += ","; }
  cur += s;
  bytes: vector<u8> = [];
  for c in cur { bytes += [c as u8 ?? 0]; }
  if !write_bytes(trace_path(), bytes) { println("trace write failed"); }
}
fn drain() -> text {
  f = file(trace_path());
  cur = f.content() ?? "";
  empty: vector<u8> = [];
  if !write_bytes(trace_path(), empty) { println("trace clear failed"); }
  return cur;
}

struct PB { id: integer }
fn OpDrop(self: PB) { note("{self.id}"); }
fn mk(id: integer) -> PB { PB { id: id } }

fn main() {
  drain();
  // `a` is a fresh null every pass: a `PB` declares `OpDrop` and no `OpCopy`, so binding `x`
  // from it MOVES it (`(H-Copy-Refuse)`), and one `a` declared before the loop was read after
  // that move on the next pass — refused as `read-after-move` once #1568's lease rules met
  // this cell.  The site, the loop and the pooled buffer are the same.
  for i in 0..3 { a: PB? = null; x = a ?? mk(2 + i); note("alive{x.id}"); }
  got = drain();
  assert(got == "alive2,2,alive3,3,alive4,4", "one record per pass, released once: {got}");
  println("pooled buffer ok");
}
